  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM EDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nToday and Tonight...The regional radar mosaic reveals scattered \nshowers and storms along the instability gradient from E TN and SW \nVA back toward W KY. Hi-res guidance has continued to struggle with \nthis pattern largely due to the uncertainties at the mesoscale \nlevel. If we look further north, another MCS is tracking through \nIllinois this afternoon. This MCS should continue moving east-\nsoutheast and remain off to the north of NE TN and SW VA. However, \nadditional scattered storms may develop this afternoon and evening. \nVisible satellite imagery highlights bubbly Cu forming across much \nof the area. Forecast soundings support the potential given moderate \ninstability, high PWs, and sufficient bulk wind shear across our \narea. This could support the development of a few strong to severe \nstorms. Today's threat of severe weather is low, but a few storms \nmay be capable of producing damaging winds, primarily across SW VA \nand NE TN. These storms will also be capable of producing heavy rain \nand localized flooding impacts, but overall our flash flood threat \nwill continue to decrease heading into Friday. \n\nFriday through Early Next Week... An intensifying ridge axis will \ncontinue to build into the Tennessee Valley ahead of the weekend.\nThis should limit any additional MCS impacts through the weekend,\nbut more typical Summer diurnally driven storm chances remain in \nplace. The ridge will support a warming trend through early next \nweek. Our temperatures should warm well into the 90s in the \nvalleys. Heat indices will approach Heat Advisory-level across our\nsoutheastern TN counties on Friday. There is some uncertainty \nwith how high the surface dew points will go Friday afternoon. \nSome guidance keeps them in the low 70s while others warm them \ninto the upper 70s. A Heat Advisory appears more likely Saturday \nand Sunday, especially across SE TN, where heat indices will warm \ninto the mid to upper 100s. This heat will peak Sunday & Monday \nbefore the ridging responsible is suppressed further west toward \nthe SW CONUS. Temperatures cool back toward normal values after \nMonday as mid- level troughing sets up over the Great Lakes and NE\nCONUS. This will allow for northwestern flow aloft to return with\nbetter daily shower & storm chances mid-week.",
